Why the error disappeared  
Next.js tries to read whatever path you give it on the server. When the path starts with “/public/…”, Node thinks you’re asking for a real directory named “public”, hits it, and fails with EISDIR.  
Removing “/public” makes the path resolve correctly to an actual file served by Next.js’s static-asset handler.

You can now refresh the preview of “/”; the site will load without the EISDIR crash.

